EXT. ABANDONED GREENHOUSE - OVERGROWN LOT - DAY

Sun slants through cracked panes. Vines thick as arms pour over rusted tables. A pigeon coos from a rafter. Somewhere in the weeds, a hose drips onto dry earth.

A BOY, 14, thin, pale, in a stained basketball jersey, crouches among shattered terracotta pots, sorting shards into a little wall. He builds quietly, tongue between his teeth. The wall is already knee-high.

A GIRL, 14, in cutoffs and a straw hat, steps through a gap in the glass, carrying a rusty watering can. She stops. Looks at him. Looks at the wall.

GIRL
That's mine.

BOY
It's nobody's.

GIRL
I built the first row.

BOY
You left.

GIRL
I was sick.

BOY
For three weeks.

GIRL
I had the flu.

BOY
Then you shouldn't have started something you couldn't finish.

She puts down the watering can. Crosses to the wall. Runs a finger along the top row, feeling the sharpness of the shards.

GIRL
You got the angles wrong.

BOY
They're not wrong.

GIRL
They're crooked.

BOY
It's called character.

She laughs, short, surprised. He allows a tiny smile. She sits across from him on an overturned bucket.

GIRL
I'm Soledad.

BOY
I know.

SOLEDAD
How?

BOY
It's on your bag.

She looks down at the canvas satchel at her hip. Stitched across the flap in yellow thread: SOLEDAD R.

SOLEDAD
Oh. Right. And you?

BOY
Kieran.

SOLEDAD
Kieran what?

KIERAN
Just Kieran.

SOLEDAD
Okay, just Kieran.

She picks up a shard of blue ceramic. Hands it to him.

SOLEDAD (CONT'D)
Put that one in the middle. It'll catch the light.

He takes it. Places it carefully. Sun filters through, and the shard glows, a little blue window in the wall of broken things.

KIERAN
Huh.

SOLEDAD
See?

KIERAN
Okay. You were right about the middle.

SOLEDAD
I'm right about a lot.

KIERAN
That's what I'm afraid of.

They work in silence for a while, passing shards back and forth, building the wall higher. A breeze stirs the vines. The pigeon watches from above.

SOLEDAD
Why do you come here?

KIERAN
It's quiet.

SOLEDAD
It's quiet at the library.

KIERAN
The library has people.

SOLEDAD
So do I, apparently.

KIERAN
Yeah. You're sort of an exception.

A long pause. She sets down the shard in her hand.

SOLEDAD
My dad's moving us in September.

KIERAN
Where?

SOLEDAD
Phoenix.

KIERAN
That's far.

SOLEDAD
It's two states. I looked.

KIERAN
Will you come back?

SOLEDAD
I don't know. Probably not.

Kieran stares at the half-built wall. His hands, which were so careful, are slack now.

KIERAN
Then why'd you come back to finish the first row?

SOLEDAD
I don't know that either.

The hose drips. A bee drones in the vines. For a moment they simply sit, the wall between them, the afternoon holding.

Then Kieran gets up. Walks to the far end of the greenhouse. Brings back a small glass jar. Inside, a single green sprout, thin, pale, reaching.

KIERAN
Found it last week. Growing through the floor.

She takes the jar. Holds it up to the light. The sprout seems to glow.

SOLEDAD
It's a tomato.

KIERAN
How do you know?

SOLEDAD
Leaf shape. See the little hairs?

KIERAN
Oh.

SOLEDAD
It's from the old crop. Someone grew them here, years ago, and it's been waiting.

KIERAN
Waiting for what?

SOLEDAD
Whoever came along.

She hands him the jar. He cradles it with both hands.

KIERAN
Then I guess it's ours.

SOLEDAD
For the summer.

KIERAN
For the summer.

He sets the jar on top of the wall, in the middle, in the blue glow, and steps back to look.

The sun moves. The wall shimmers. A shadow falls across it, long and thin, from the gap in the glass where nobody stands.

Kieran turns. Soledad is watching the gap. Her hat has slipped to the back of her head.

SOLEDAD
(quiet)
Did you see that?

KIERAN
It's only the vines.

SOLEDAD
Right.

Neither of them moves. The shadow shifts. A pigeon launches from the rafters in a clatter of wings.

They both laugh, shaky, relieved, and then the laughter fades and they are two kids in a ruined place, holding very still.
